The Go rendering: one module whose module file declares two inert directives
about one module that exists and that no file imports. The requirement is
direct and no package of the target imports a package the module provides, so
the requirement is unbacked; the replace names that same module, which provides
no package the load reaches, so the directive redirects nothing. The two
subjects are the two lines the directives are written on. Nothing in the module
imports anything outside the standard library, so the rendering loads with no
network access and the required module is never fetched.

-- fixture.json --
{
  "symbols": {
    "UnusedRequirement": { "file": "target/go.mod", "line": 5 },
    "NoopReplace": { "file": "target/go.mod", "line": 7 }
  }
}
-- target/go.mod --
module example.com/target

go 1.27.0

require golang.org/x/sync v0.16.0

replace golang.org/x/sync v0.16.0 => golang.org/x/sync v0.17.0
-- target/main.go --
// Command target imports nothing, so the module its module file requires and
// replaces provides no package the load reaches.
package main

func main() {}
